roblem "For the curve $y=\\frac{3}{4}{(\\frac{1}{5})}^{x}+2,$ find the point of intersection with the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\n- For the curve $y={(\\frac{1}{5})}^{x},$ the point of intersection with the $y$-axis is $(0,1).$\n\n- For the scaled function $\\frac{3}{4}{(\\frac{1}{5})}^{x},$ the $y$-coordinate becomes\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{3}{4}\\cdot 1=\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\n- Finally, after a vertical shift of $2$ units up, we get a point with the $y$-coordinate\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{3}{4}+2=\\frac{11}{4}.\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nTo find the $y$-intercept, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{3}{4}{(\\frac{1}{5})}^{0}+2 \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}\\cdot 1+2 \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}+2 \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=\\frac{3}{4}{(\\frac{1}{5})}^{x}+2$ intercepts the $y$-axis at $(0,\\frac{11}{4}).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-31363/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "617f0fe6-7998-5d77-b11f-55a0eca11440"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-31363"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-55433"] :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote of the curve $y=k{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{x}+c$ is $y=c.$ In the graph above, the horizontal asymptote is $y=-3,$ hence $c=-3.$\n\nWe can solve for $k$ by finding a point on the graph and substituting for $x$ and $y.$ The labeled point on the graph is $(0,3),$ so we substitute $x=0$ and $y=3$ into the function and solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}+c & =y \\\\ k{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}-3 & =y \\\\ k{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{0}-3 & =3 \\\\ k-3 & =3 \\\\ k & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\nk-c=6-(-3)=9.\n$$\n\nThe equation of the curve is $y=6{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}-3.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-170349/selection/3618eeb4de8678762c18ec5049c75bcda565421300d239b77add42255368db12" :answer/id #uuid "7bbc8c57-260f-5e69-a8d9-816990a3462e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(0,\\frac{13}{10})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-170349/selection/49dd955f7246aaaf8b0009bb70935ca10c0397c18703e7d61cbb9a56e3d7ae6f" :answer/id #uuid "333e2172-7ae1-5a82-bf98-851d5991c9a6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(0,1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-170349/selection/9a1335eafa45146640b35d0c4768b221d33bebad9c7b0c84fd4aa4792a5d8b13" :answer/id #uuid "1eb2a3c9-b515-566c-abff-cd0e276887f5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(0,\\frac{21}{10})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-170349/selection/e7bcb1bd13f74f4be5a671b49043dd50499a478fcec3bbdb37ecc7d1d735c37e" :answer/id #uuid "04d2139a-e33e-5a31-a9a5-e2a1e1e1733d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(0,\\frac{5}{2})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-170349/selection/150d99653fc2555ef55da6bf23626f59964f3ca8ca854ad3e4e5141679d4b999" :answer/id #uuid "1f94b193-985e-5fa4-93e9-cbbbb376f300" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(0,\\frac{1}{2})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-170349/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "1ec24b9b-d970-5b5f-9636-4936c9b671f8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-170349/selection/e7bcb1bd13f74f4be5a671b49043dd50499a478fcec3bbdb37ecc7d1d735c37e" :answer-field/choices ["q-170349/selection/3618eeb4de8678762c18ec5049c75bcda565421300d239b77add42255368db12" "q-170349/selection/49dd955f7246aaaf8b0009bb70935ca10c0397c18703e7d61cbb9a56e3d7ae6f" "q-170349/selection/9a1335eafa45146640b35d0c4768b221d33bebad9c7b0c84fd4aa4792a5d8b13" "q-170349/selection/e7bcb1bd13f74f4be5a671b49043dd50499a478fcec3bbdb37ecc7d1d735c37e" "q-170349/selection/150d99653fc2555ef55da6bf23626f59964f3ca8ca854ad3e4e5141679d4b999"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-170349" :question/id #uuid "d4172d49-9de6-50d7-a291-6f9452de375f" :question/math-academy-id "q-170349" :question/is-example false :question/problem "For the curve $y=2{(\\frac{4}{5})}^{x}+\\frac{1}{2},$ find the point of intersection with the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\n- For the curve $y={(\\frac{4}{5})}^{x},$ the point of intersection with the $y$-axis is $(0,1).$\n\n- For the scaled function $2{(\\frac{4}{5})}^{x},$ the $y$-coordinate becomes\n\n$$\ny=2\\cdot 1=2.\n$$\n\n- Finally, after a vertical shift of $\\frac{1}{2}$ units up, we get a point with the $y$-coordinate\n\n$$\ny=2+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{5}{2}.\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nTo find the $y$-intercept, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2{(\\frac{4}{5})}^{0}+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =2+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=2{(\\frac{4}{5})}^{x}+\\frac{1}{2}$ intercepts the $y$-axis at $(0,\\frac{5}{2}).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-170349/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "617f0fe6-7998-5d77-b11f-55a0eca11440"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-170349"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]}]
